---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";

import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LiveTimes from "../components/LiveTimes.astro";
import { SITE, SOURCES } from "../lib/site";

import widgets from "../assets/widgets.jpeg";
import widgetsDark from "../assets/widgets-dark.jpeg";
import iphoneWidget from "../assets/iphone-widget.jpeg";
import main from "../assets/screen-main-1.jpeg";
import mainDark from "../assets/screen-main-dark-1.jpeg";
import qibla from "../assets/screen-qibla.jpeg";

const shots = [
  {
    src: main,
    alt: "The app showing today's seven prayer times for Gombak, Selangor",
  },
  {
    src: widgets,
    alt: "Four simplesolat widget styles on an Android home screen",
  },
  {
    src: iphoneWidget,
    alt: "The simplesolat widget on an iPhone home screen, showing the current prayer and a countdown to the next",
  },
  { src: mainDark, alt: "The app in dark mode" },
  { src: widgetsDark, alt: "The widgets in dark mode" },
  { src: qibla, alt: "The Qibla compass" },
];
---

<Base>
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>simplesolat</h1>
    <p class="tagline">{SITE.tagline}</p>
    <p>
      A free prayer times app for Android and iPhone. Home screen widgets that
      follow you when you travel, reminders for the prayers you choose, and all
      seven times, including Syuruk. No ads, no account, and your location never
      leaves your phone.
    </p>
    <p class="actions">
      <a class="button" href={SITE.playStore}>
        Get it on Google Play
      </a>
      <a class="button" href={SITE.appStore}>
        Download on the App Store
      </a>
      <a class="button secondary" href="#times">
        See today's times
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <div id="times">
    <LiveTimes />
  </div>

  <h2 id="features">What it does</h2>
  <ul class="features">
    <li>
      <strong>Widgets that update themselves.</strong> Several styles, light and
      dark, and on iPhone the Lock Screen too. They refresh in the background,
      and when you travel into a new zone, they switch to it on their own. No
      opening the app at the start of each month.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>All seven times.</strong> Imsak, Fajr, Syuruk, Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib
      and Isha, with the current one highlighted. Syuruk tells you when the time
      for Fajr ends. In Malaysia and Brunei you also get Dhuha, from the
      official timetable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reminders</strong> when each prayer time begins, for the prayers
      you choose, including Imsak, Syuruk and Dhuha.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Today's times in your notifications.</strong> On Android, an
      optional pinned notification shows the five prayer times and a countdown
      to the next.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A Qibla compass.</strong>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Works offline.</strong> Timetables are kept on your phone for
      weeks ahead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Works in many countries.</strong> Official timetables for nine
      countries, and calculated times everywhere else.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Free, with no ads and no account.</strong> The code is
      <a href={SITE.github}>public on GitHub</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="shots" role="list">
    {shots.map((s) => (
      <div role="listitem">
        <Image src={s.src} alt={s.alt} widths={[240, 480]} sizes="240px" />
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="sources">Where the times come from</h2>
  <p>
    Wherever there's an official timetable, simplesolat uses it, taken from each
    country's religious authority:
  </p>
  <table class="sources">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Country</th>
        <th>Official source</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{s.country}</td>
          <td>
            <a href={s.url}>{s.authority}</a>
          </td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Everywhere else, the app calculates the times on your phone, using the
    method that's usual in your country, and the prayer times above do the same
    in your browser. Zone boundaries come from
    <a href="https://www.geoboundaries.org/">geoBoundaries</a>, and country
    borders from <a href="https://www.naturalearthdata.com/">Natural Earth</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
  <p>
    simplesolat works out your prayer zone <strong>on your phone</strong>, by
    checking your location against zone boundaries it has downloaded. Your
    location is never sent anywhere. The only thing the app asks a server for is
    the timetable for your zone.
  </p>
  <p>
    There's no account, no analytics and no ads. Read the
    <a href="/privacy/">full privacy policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="help">Help</h2>
  <p>
    Reminders late, the widget not updating, or the wrong zone? The
    <a href="/support/">support page</a> has fixes for the common problems, and
    how to contact me.
  </p>

  <h2 id="developers">For developers</h2>
  <p>
    The prayer times behind simplesolat are open and free to use in your own app
    or website:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={SITE.data}>simplesolat-data</a>: the official timetables, zones
      and zone boundaries as static files on a CDN, MIT licensed. This page reads
      them straight from your browser.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={SITE.apiDocs}>simplesolat API</a>: the same data as a simple JSON
      API at <code>api.simplesolat.com</code>. Find the zone for a GPS location,
      then get its times for a day, a month or a year. No key needed, and you
      can run your own copy.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Spotted a wrong time or zone? Please
    <a href={SITE.dataIssues}>report it on GitHub</a>, or send a fix. Keeping
    the data accurate is easier together.
  </p>

  <h2 id="story">Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    simplesolat started after the free prayer app I relied on stopped working,
    and I found out why.
    <a href={SITE.story}>Read the story</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-top: 2.5rem;
  }
  .tagline {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    margin: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }
  .features {
    padding-left: 1.1rem;
  }
  .features li + li {
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
  .shots {
    display: flex;
    gap: 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: 1.5rem 0.5rem;
    scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
  }
  .shots > div {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    scroll-snap-align: start;
  }
  .shots :global(img) {
    width: 240px;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }
  .sources {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .sources th,
  .sources td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.4rem 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }
</style>
